WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.522
OR represents alternatives. A row remains when at least one condition is true.

00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:12.282
In this slide, a patient qualifies when the diagnosis is Pneumonia, Flu, or Asthma.

00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:15.822
The patient does not need to have all three diagnoses.

00:00:15.822 --> 00:00:18.557
Any one of those values is enough.

00:00:18.557 --> 00:00:26.397
If several OR conditions compare the same column with a set of values, IN can often make the rule easier to

00:00:26.397 --> 00:00:32.142
read. Instead of repeating diagnosis equals Pneumonia OR diagnosis equals Flu OR diagnosis equals Asthma,

00:00:32.142 --> 00:00:34.887
we can say diagnosis IN this list.

00:00:34.887 --> 00:00:37.857
Think about a customer-service project.

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:44.597
A case may be considered urgent if the customer is a VIP, OR the complaint is about fraud,

00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:50.502
OR the issue has been open for more than forty-eight hours.

00:00:50.502 --> 00:00:53.752
Different paths can lead to the same outcome.

00:00:53.752 --> 00:00:56.627
OR often broadens the result.

00:00:56.627 --> 00:01:01.417
If one condition returns ten rows and you add another OR condition,

00:01:01.417 --> 00:01:08.492
the combined result will normally contain the original rows plus any new rows that satisfy the second

00:01:08.492 --> 00:01:12.772
condition. The challenge begins when OR and AND are mixed.

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:14.137
For example, active AND high risk OR senior.

00:01:14.137 --> 00:01:19.472
Does active apply to both groups or only to high risk?

00:01:19.472 --> 00:01:24.392
That depends on grouping and operator precedence.

00:01:24.392 --> 00:01:24.712
So OR by itself is simple.

00:01:24.712 --> 00:01:31.227
The important skill is recognizing which conditions are alternatives and making those alternatives visually

00:01:31.227 --> 00:01:35.864
clear when the rule becomes more complex.
